A gnome warlock named Tinkwizz set out through Stranglethorn Vale, his trusty Succubus, Lira, shimmering beside him. As they crept past thorn bushes and wary trolls, Lira’s seductive charm disarmed a few, while Tinkwizz’s wild casting sent shadows dancing. When a pack of wolves cornered them, Lira’s Soothing Kiss calmed the lead beast, letting Tinkwizz unleash a burst of shadow damage. Later, while foraging for mushrooms, Lira used Lesser Invisibility, letting Tinkwizz nab a rare fungus without incident. But as they approached the ancient Ruins, a spectral voice whispered, “You seek power, but fear the void’s call.” Tinkwizz hesitated—Voidwalkers offered protection and healing, but Lira’s crowd control and utility shone brighter for quests. In the end, Tinkwizz realized that while the void’s might is tempting, the Succubus’s versatility made them the better companion for the wilds of Azeroth.

Answer: Most warlocks use voidwalkers for their threat and healing, but succubi are better for leveling due to superior crowd control, utility spells (like invisibility), and easier soloing, making them more practical for questing despite the voidwalker’s advantages in combat. [end of text]


